WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.290 --> 00:00:03.610
Simple features beat most AI control monitors. Here's the setup.

2
00:00:03.710 --> 00:00:06.090
An AI agent slips two lines into a file.

3
00:00:06.050 --> 00:00:09.470
A monitor reads the problem and the code, and scores how suspicious it looks.

4
00:00:10.316 --> 00:00:14.816
Score every solution, and a good monitor pulls the honest and backdoored piles apart.

5
00:00:15.116 --> 00:00:18.076
These are twenty four published monitors, from best to worst.

6
00:00:18.216 --> 00:00:20.656
Now add a word counter that never runs the code.

7
00:00:20.576 --> 00:00:21.896
It beats twenty of them.

8
00:00:23.115 --> 00:00:24.175
Why does that work?

9
00:00:24.475 --> 00:00:26.515
Each pile was written by a different author.

10
00:00:26.635 --> 00:00:29.635
People write compact code, and models add spaces.

11
00:00:29.655 --> 00:00:32.395
And one in seven test backdoors can never even trigger.

12
00:00:32.803 --> 00:00:36.083
Keep the problems but swap the authors, and the score follows them.

13
00:00:36.083 --> 00:00:39.483
With backdoors by Claude Sonnet 5, it rises to point nine five.

14
00:00:39.623 --> 00:00:42.463
When Sonnet 5 writes both sides, it drops to point seven five.

15
00:00:42.974 --> 00:00:47.534
So we rebuilt the benchmark with one author, and checked that every backdoor works.

16
00:00:47.774 --> 00:00:51.774
A Sonnet 5 monitor catches seventy seven percent of the older models' backdoors.

17
00:00:51.774 --> 00:00:54.614
Of its own, it catches only thirty seven percent.

18
00:00:54.694 --> 00:00:57.854
Count only the ones that work, and that rises to sixty one. Still fewer.

19
00:00:58.463 --> 00:01:02.813
So before you trust a monitor benchmark, report baselines that never run the code,

20
00:01:02.513 --> 00:01:06.863
pair each class with code by another author, and check that every backdoor works.

21
00:01:07.183 --> 00:01:11.463
Because a high score on a benchmark is not, by itself, evidence of sabotage detection.

